{:edb/db-before-t 1470 :edb/db-after-t 1471 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534783 50 #inst "2026-10-12T13:12:42.692Z" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186128515 1078 #edb/ref 17592186128517 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186128988 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186128988 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435896 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435897 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129327 1050 420.0 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129837 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/d7/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129837 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/d7/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)" 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186129838 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/43/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3\\}.\n$$" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129838 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/43/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}.\n$$" 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186129839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129839 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/3f/3fccfbd6fd95005958fc9c9c14a1d327c27ef21351b1a32f3a5da553fdbf1539.png)" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128527 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186129841 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=-3?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186129841 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=-3?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129841 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/72/726ad8599b89029d3dba8e79ca947af99c17bd8712e551e49fc33d3e597a5cb7.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-y}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186129841 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/72/726ad8599b89029d3dba8e79ca947af99c17bd8712e551e49fc33d3e597a5cb7.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-y}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186284608 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the triangle bounded between the lines $y=1-x,$ $y=x-1,$ and $x=0?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186284608 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the triangle bounded between the lines $y=1-x,$ $y=x-1,$ and $x=0?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186284608 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-x\\, & ⟹\\,x=1-y \\\\ y=x-1\\, & ⟹\\,x=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/27/27503f983e3e02fa748bb8ef32966a6cd3b5fff29082ffaf3c10d0521b630e93.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1-|y|\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186284608 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-x\\, & ⟹\\,x=1-y \\\\ y=x-1\\, & ⟹\\,x=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/27/27503f983e3e02fa748bb8ef32966a6cd3b5fff29082ffaf3c10d0521b630e93.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336034 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337200 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337489 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337829 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338117 13194139534783 17592186045417 false] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435882 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435886 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435889 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435890 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186338641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435892 