86435894 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435896 1069 "q-72139" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435896 1071 "![](images/2c/2cd94016388c0c128c6b2997fc9c04563f73b7024436b219f4adccc42640a331.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435896 1072 #uuid "df61b368-91ad-5f51-9082-c280e6c8c895" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435896 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=(2-y)^{3}\\,⟹\\,y=2-\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nThe function $y=\\sqrt{x}+2$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/95/95c5a0ddac341f0787fa020df5e04096287262ddc85441a93712bdaeee94eb0a.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=2-\\sqrt[3]{x}$ and leaves through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{x}+2.$ So, we have $2-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}+2.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}+2\\}.\n$$" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435895 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1069 "q-168840" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1071 "![](images/99/99c16d2b48da841b9ba6f3bc400701b26af36c8780d27293ddb68d433c27da3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1072 #uuid "ed99a179-e753-5cd2-8fc5-79553f622379" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true] [17592186435897 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-1}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9c46e1fa6cc373896c2905aab4c1ceca6ff4de7cd498297d4a02e5c4375a39.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x={e}^{y-1}.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le {e}^{y-1}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {e}^{y-1}\\}.\n$$" 13194139534783 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0747cf82-408b-58c2-b5d7-dcefd0ed68ff" 17592186435869 "answer-14ceafca-0ef0-5d60-bc6c-15fd2f018b65" 17592186435870 "answer-17567c73-9403-53e0-b46b-a5ceadd85060" 17592186435871 "answer-1c956e41-28d5-5cb3-80b8-79cc5871e46a" 17592186435872 "answer-25d88735-5257-5296-9c68-7e1d6906875f" 17592186435873 "answer-3a5bd8aa-e2c6-5754-937f-9b6671d7c03f" 17592186435874 "answer-429ef4b8-fbc0-567d-af67-084931824074" 17592186435875 "answer-4b704cda-a7e0-5c8b-9cf6-66429dfd1ade" 17592186435876 "answer-6575688f-64bd-5a5c-b00c-b61181f473fc" 17592186435877 "answer-6f70b707-beb3-5c61-9042-eb57a0ba9c3